=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that the two halves of the network have become completely isolated — is not confirmed by any live measurement. A full topology-wide reachability test shows pc0 (20.1.1.10) reaching pc12 (120.1.1.10) and vice versa at 0% packet loss, directly contradicting the claim of a network "divorce."

A complete broad-search pass was executed: Phase A (infra_sweep, l2_snapshot) found no ACL rules, no addressing anomalies, no duplicate MACs on active interfaces, and no link faults. Phase B (ospf_snapshot, tc_snapshot) surfaced OSPF flags on r8, r12, and r13, all of which resolved to intentional topology design rather than faults. Specifically: r8 is an OSPF ABR whose area 0.0.0.2 is a local stub (segment BB has no peer routers) and whose virtual link to r4 through transit area 0.0.0.1 is operating in Full state; r12 and r13 are EIGRP 100 routers, not OSPF participants — their lack of OSPF network statements is correct configuration, not misconfiguration; r11 bridges the EIGRP domain (r12, r13) to the OSPF backbone and the end-to-end path is functional. Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no resource exhaustion on any device. There is no web service layer in this topology.

Root cause: No anomaly exists. The network is operating correctly across both routing domains. The ticket is unsubstantiated by live evidence; likely based on a transient observation, a misread monitoring alert, or a misidentified test during maintenance.

Fix: No corrective action required. If specific additional paths need verification beyond the pc0↔pc12 probe, targeted ping tests from intermediate routers can be run to confirm.
=== END ===